<Plain
  title="Privacy policy · OpenAlarm"
  description="How OpenAlarm stores settings and uses app permissions."
  heading="Privacy policy"
>
  <p>OpenAlarm does not collect personal data or send it to the developer. There are no OpenAlarm accounts, analytics, tracking, advertising, or third-party data services.</p>

  <p>Your individual alarms and scheduling state stay on your device, plus the device backups you control.</p>

  <p>If you are signed in to iCloud, OpenAlarm stores your global alarm and nap settings in your private iCloud key-value store. This lets the app restore settings after a reinstall. The backup includes snooze, wake-up check, challenge, ringtone, volume, and nap defaults. It does not include your alarm list. You can disable iCloud for OpenAlarm in the system settings.</p>

  <p>Permissions the app asks for are used only for the feature they enable: notifications and alarms so your alarm can ring, Motion &amp; Fitness for the Steps challenge, device motion for the Shake challenge, and the camera for the Scan challenge. Camera frames are processed on-device and never stored or uploaded.</p>

  <p>This website is a static page and sets no cookies.</p>

  <p>Questions? Open an issue on GitHub (<a href="https://github.com/gretzke/open-alarm/issues">github.com/gretzke/open-alarm/issues</a>) or email <a href="mailto:support@tryopenalarm.com">support@tryopenalarm.com</a>.</p>
</Plain>
